From ff0a446f9c3197c53abd1cdecce599a7f1fb267c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:32:35 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] CUDA: sm_121 wide Q4_K mmvq vec_dot (vdr 4)

Decode on this track is 91% quantized matvec and ~71% of that time is Q4_K
(ffn gate+up fused, ffn down, conv in/out projections, attention q/o). A
standalone read-bandwidth probe puts GB10's achievable DRAM read rate at
251.6 GB/s for exactly the mmvq access geometry (one 128-thread block per
~1.1 KB row); the stock Q4_K matvec runs its weights at ~205 GB/s. The gap
is issue-side: at the stock vdr = 2 every lane fetches its weights as two
separate 4-byte loads.

This adds an sm_121 mmvq parameter table whose only entries are Q4_K vdr = 4
and Q4_K nwarps = 2, plus the sm_121 vec_dot_q4_K_q8_1 that vdr = 4 needs.
One call now covers the column pair (iqs, iqs+2): the pair shares bq8_offset,
the unpacked scale/min pair and d8, so that work happens once instead of
twice, and each weight fetch becomes an 8-byte int2 load. block_q4_K is 144
bytes with qs at offset 16 and a row is a whole number of blocks, so qs is
16-byte aligned and q4 + i0 (i0 in {0, 2}) is 8-byte aligned - the int2 load
is well formed. The q8_1 activation side deliberately keeps 4-byte loads:
block_q8_1 is 36 bytes, so its qs is only 4-byte aligned at odd block
indices.

nwarps drops 4 -> 2 for Q4_K purely to keep coverage: blocks_per_iter is
vdr*nwarps*warp_size/qi, so vdr 2->4 with nwarps 4->2 leaves it at 8 - the
same single k-loop pass over the 8 blocks of a 2048-column row that stock
makes. Every other type and every other ncols_dst mirrors GENERIC exactly.

Measured on the runner box, 5 interleaved whole-process rounds against a
series-only control build (cross-tree no-op floor measured at 0.9996 first):
tg128 112.29-112.46 -> 120.95-121.14, 5/5 arms disjoint, ratio 1.0776.
pp512 neutral (8002-8057 vs 7967-8108, overlapping) - as expected, prefill
takes MMQ and never enters this kernel.

